| `tests/integration/test_exit_gate.py` | Fails an exit written outside `scripts/exit_codes.py`, an entry block or `main` whose status cannot be read as an `ExitCode` member, a `passthrough` handed the script's own status, an `ExitCode` member the limits guide's table does not name, a `fail` or `finish` call outside the entry block of a module another module imports, and a shell script that does not source `scripts/exit_codes.sh` or ends other than through `exit "$EXIT_<NAME>"`, `exit_fail` or `exit_passthrough` over a wrapped tool's status, at its file and line, so every process ends through one helper with a named status. | `make test` | [`import_layers.may_end_the_process.<name>`](settings.md#configprojectjson), [`expiring_tier.expires`](settings.md#configprojectjson), [`shell.no_scripts`](settings.md#configprojectjson) | No shell script is a finding unless `shell.no_scripts` gives the reason, and with `shell.enabled` false no shell script is judged. In a generated project the exit and shared-module findings judge through `scripts/expiring_tier.py`, as the config-reads gate's do. It cannot see an exit reached through `getattr`, `os.abort`, `os.kill` or an uncaught exception. Not the table's writer: `make exit-codes` writes it and `make check-exit-codes` gates it. | [python-style.md](../guides/python-style.md) | always |
